---
import CaseStudy from "~/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseHero from "~/components/case/CaseHero.astro";
import CaseLead from "~/components/case/CaseLead.astro";
import Hood from "~/components/case/Hood.astro";
import NextNav from "~/components/case/NextNav.astro";
import Scenes from "~/components/case/Scenes.astro";
import SceneStep from "~/components/case/SceneStep.astro";
import Section from "~/components/ui/Section.astro";

const hood = [
  {
    icon: "aws-AmazonElasticKubernetesService",
    name: "EKS and AKS (Azure)",
    what: "the same Kubernetes setup in every cluster",
  },
  { name: "Karpenter", what: "adds and removes nodes with the load" },
  { name: "cert-manager", what: "automatic TLS certificates" },
  {
    icon: "aws-AmazonAurora",
    name: "Aurora, Redis, OpenSearch",
    what: "ready-made modules a project picks from",
  },
  {
    icon: "aws-AmazonCloudFront",
    name: "CloudFront",
    what: "one entry, routing to Ireland or Tokyo by path",
  },
  {
    icon: "aws-AmazonSimpleStorageService",
    name: "Velero",
    what: "cluster backups, and we’ve rehearsed a restore into another cluster",
  },
] as const;
const icons = hood.flatMap((h) => ("icon" in h ? [h.icon] : []));
---

<CaseStudy
  project="hosting-platform"
  title="Hosting platform · Guus Ekkelenkamp"
  og="hosting-platform"
  description="Case study: the Kubernetes platform I built at Harborn for our client projects, on AWS and Azure."
  icons={icons}
>
  <CaseHero
    project="hosting-platform"
    meta="Work at Harborn · 2022 to now · I designed and built it, and lead the team that runs it"
    title="Hosting platform"
    line="The Kubernetes platform that runs our client projects on AWS and Azure."
  />
  <CaseLead>
    <p>
      In 2022 every client project had its own pipelines, monitoring and access, so we worked out every
      upgrade and every incident from scratch. I wanted one platform for all of them, so I started building
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today it runs 8 Kubernetes clusters on AWS in Ireland and Tokyo and on Azure, hosting more than 300
      Dutch and international clients with over 800 deployments. The largest cluster runs more than 1,500
      pods. A new client gets the same modules and network as everyone else and deploys the same way.
    </p>
  </CaseLead>
  <Scenes id="story" title="How the platform works">
    <SceneStep scene="standard">
      <h3>Every project on the same setup</h3>
      <p>New projects get the same setup as the existing ones, so one fix or upgrade covers all of them.</p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="scale">
      <h3>It scales with the load</h3>
      <p>
        Production runs across three availability zones, behind a load balancer with a fixed public IP per
        zone, so clients can allowlist us. Karpenter adds nodes when the load goes up and removes them when it
        drops. Its permissions are pinned to one node role, so adding a node group can’t widen them.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="regions">
      <h3>Ireland and Tokyo behind CloudFront</h3>
      <p>
        CloudFront sends region-specific paths to Tokyo and everything else to Ireland, and static content
        comes straight from the edge. Tokyo cuts latency for our customers in Asia; it isn’t a failover.
        Ireland stays the one place where we manage things.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="ipv6">
      <h3>The move to IPv6</h3>
      <p>
        We were running out of IPv4 addresses, and EKS runs a cluster on IPv4 or on IPv6, not both. So we
        moved our hosting clusters on EKS to IPv6-only. Anything that only speaks IPv4 is reached through
        DNS64 and NAT64.
      </p>
      <p>
        I led the move. Internal traffic from the pods to private services, such as asset storage, databases
        and Redis, also went through NAT64, which made it very expensive. Some older client sites, built
        before IPv6 mattered, didn’t work on it. We went into hypercare, fixed every issue and never went back
        to IPv4.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="learned">
      <h3>One codebase for the AWS clusters</h3>
      <p>
        The AWS clusters come from the same Terraform and OpenTofu modules, so we make and review a change
        once and roll it out to them one after another. New infrastructure delivery is moving to Pulumi.
      </p>
      <p>Some clients are on Azure. They get the same platform on AKS, with a few changes.</p>
    </SceneStep>
  </Scenes>
  <Section id="hood" title="Under the hood">
    <Hood items={[...hood]} />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        New infrastructure is Pulumi in Python, and only the pipeline applies it. How a change gets there is
        in
        <a href="/projects/infrastructure-delivery/">infrastructure delivery</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        App delivery is moving to Argo CD, which deploys acceptance from release candidates and production
        only from stable release tags. We’re rolling out onboarding by GitHub topic, so that a repo that
        follows our conventions only needs one topic to be deployed. On the clusters that run Alloy, pod logs
        go to Loki through the logging pipeline I built.
      </p>
      <p>
        A small CronJob we built keeps chosen Deployments, ConfigMaps and Secrets in Tokyo in step with the
        main cluster, so a change is still made in one place. We publish a list of the database, search and
        ingress versions we support, with an end-of-support date for the older ones, so we can plan upgrades
        well before they’re due.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
  <Section id="lessons" title="Lessons" bodyClass="prose">
    <p>Before a network change I now map where internal traffic goes and what it costs.</p>
    <p>
      The Tokyo syncer predates our move to GitOps. Today I’d replace it with Argo CD ApplicationSets that
      target both clusters, so git is the only source for both regions.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <NextNav
    prev={{ href: "/projects/", small: "Back to", label: "all projects" }}
    next={{
      href: "/projects/infrastructure-delivery/",
      small: "Next project",
      label: "Infrastructure delivery",
      arrow: "right",
    }}
  />
</CaseStudy>
